 "equipment_type": "Fan coil unit controller (room thermostat)",
 "applies_to": "Schneider Electric SE8000 Series touchscreen terminal equipment controllers for fan coils: SER8300/SER8350 (shown with the SC3000 relay pack) and SE8300/SE8350, as covered by the February 2014 BACnet integration manual.",
 "summary": "The SE8300 and SER8300 are SE8000 Series touchscreen fan coil controllers on BACnet MS/TP (B-ASC). This 2014 manual prints one object list with tick columns for four models: SER8300, SER8350, SE8300 and SE8350. The 8350 models add humidity sensing and dehumidification objects; the SE models add universal outputs UO 9-12, remote sensor inputs and an auxiliary output, while the SER models have dedicated heating/cooling outputs. Object names are those of this older firmware and differ from the later SpaceLogic SE8350 guides.",
 "bacnet_transport": [
  "BACnet MS/TP"
 ],
 "firmware": null,
 "default_device_instance": "83000 + MAC address",
 "access_inferred": false,
 "notes": [
  "Model availability is given at the end of a description when an object is not on all four models (SER8300, SER8350, SE8300, SE8350).",
  "Access follows the manual's property tables: AV, AO, BV, BO and MV present values are commandable, CSVs are writable, and AI, BI and MSI (MI here) objects are read-only.",
  "BI 29, BI 30, BI 91, MV 46 and MV 47 are printed with both names (e.g. 'BI 1 / UI 16 Status'): the part before the slash applies to SER83xx models, after the slash to SE83xx models.",
  "The range tables use a few alternative names (e.g. Unoccupied Cooling Setpoint, RH Display for MV 70, Fan Menu Sequence for MV 57); names here follow the main object list. No state tables are printed for MV 49, 81, 91, 92 and 94, and no ranges for AV 5, 6 and 46.",
  "For the newer SpaceLogic SE8350 / SER8350 firmware (much larger object list, different names) see the separate SE8350 and SER8350 lists."
 ],
 "source": {
  "title": "SE8000 Series BACnet Integration Manual - Terminal Equipment Controller, Commercial and Lodging HVAC Applications",
  "doc_number": "IG-SE8000-BNET-US.EN.02.2014.v1",
  "revision_date": "February 2014",
  "url": "https://assets.kele.com/Catalog/22%20Thermostats_Controllers/PDFs/SE8000%20Series%20BACnet%20User%20Manual-2014.pdf",
  "unofficial": true,
  "host": "Kele"
 },
 "integration": {
  "summary": "Set a MAC address from 0 to 127 (the factory value 254 keeps BACnet off), then command occupancy (MV 10), system mode (MV 16), fan mode (MV 17) and the occupied setpoints (AV 39 / AV 40). Read room temperature (AV 100), effective occupancy (MI 33), the PI demands and the alarm BVs. Use priorities 4-16 for live commands.",
  "minimum_points": [
   {
    "ref": "MV 10",
    "name": "Occupancy Command",
    "why": "BMS occupancy: local, occupied or unoccupied"
   },
   {
    "ref": "MV 16",
    "name": "System Mode",
    "why": "Off / auto / cool / heat"
   },
   {
    "ref": "MV 17",
    "name": "Fan Mode",
    "why": "Fan mode: low / medium / high / auto / on"
   },
   {
    "ref": "AV 39",
    "name": "Occupied Heat Setpoint",
    "why": "Occupied heating setpoint"
   },
   {
    "ref": "AV 40",
    "name": "Occupied Cool Setpoint",
    "why": "Occupied cooling setpoint"
   },
   {
    "ref": "MI 33",
    "name": "Effective Occupancy",
    "why": "Actual occupancy state"
   },
   {
    "ref": "AV 100",
    "name": "Room Temperature",
    "why": "Space temperature used for control"
   },
   {
    "ref": "AO 21",
    "name": "PI Heating Demand",
    "why": "Heating demand, 0-100 %"
   },
   {
    "ref": "AO 22",
    "name": "PI Cooling Demand",
    "why": "Cooling demand, 0-100 %"
   },
   {
    "ref": "BV 37",
    "name": "Service Alarm",
    "why": "Service alarm from an input set to Service"
   }
  ],
  "enable": "BACnet communication starts once the MAC address is changed from the factory value 254 to 0-127. The BMS then writes MV 10 Occupancy Command (1 = Local Occupancy, 2 = Occupied, 3 = Unoccupied) and MV 16 System Mode.",
  "heartbeat": "No heartbeat point. If the controller goes offline, network-bound Room Temperature, Outdoor Temperature and Occupancy are released.",
  "remote_sensors": "The manual lists Outdoor Temperature (AV 101) as a global value the BMS can send to all controllers, and Room Temperature (AV 100) for testing and override. Writes at priority 4-16 override the controller's own value until relinquished or power is lost.",
  "setup": [
   "Factory MAC address is 254, which disables BACnet; set 0-127 to join the token ring.",
   "Default Device ID is 83000 + MAC (MAC 63 gives 83063); default device name is the model number plus MAC. Both are writable.",
   "Baud rate auto-detects at power-up (MV 8, default Auto); after a speed change the device keeps the old rate for 10 minutes before auto-bauding again.",
   "Up to 64 nodes per EIA-485 segment; maximum data rate used is 76800 bps."
  ],
  "gotchas": [
   "Priorities 1-3 are saved to flash and survive a power cycle, locking the value until a NULL is written at the same priority; use 4-16 (RAM) for live control and rewrite after a restart.",
   "Writing the Relinquish_Default stores a new value in flash without bypassing the controller logic; the manual prefers it for configuration and user HMI objects.",
   "CSV 1 / CSV 2 screen messages are not saved in flash and are lost on power loss.",
   "Remove configuration objects from the BAS after setup to cut polling traffic, as the manual recommends."
  ]
 },
